// RetireControl Retire a control
//
// "A CONTROL IS NEVER DELETED, ONLY RETIRED" (the Controls page). "Retiring takes it off the
// library's current list and it stops counting toward any framework: a requirement whose
// only linked control is retired counts as not done until another control is linked." It
// opens by its id and is listed with `retired=true`, saying when it was retired.
//
// WHAT STAYS, FOR THE AUDITOR: "Its evidence, its history and the findings raised against
// it." Its evidence is listed and opened as before, and a finding that names it still names
// it. What it no longer does is count: no framework's readiness and no requirement's row
// reads a retired control.
//
// A RETIRED CONTROL IS READ AND NOT CHANGED. Changing its statement or its steward, setting
// its status, linking it to a requirement or taking a link off, attaching evidence to it and
// removing a piece are each refused with `control_retired`, and nothing is written.
//
// NEVER UNDONE: "Retiring is never undone." No operation brings a control back. "A retired
// ready-made control can be picked again, as a new copy": a pick counts the controls in use
// alone, so it makes a new control, and the retired one stays as it was.
//
// RETIRING WHAT IS ALREADY RETIRED CHANGES NOTHING and answers the same control, with the
// moment it was first retired, as retiring a vendor or a policy does: a retry whose answer
// was lost is not refused.
//
// A POST AND NOT A DELETE, because the record does not go. The permission is the catalogue's
// `.delete`, which "the AI asks first". It leaves one audit event, at the control.
//
// WHAT IS NOT DONE, BECAUSE IT IS NOT BUILT: "an audit already open keeps it in the
// Statement of Applicability it froze". No audit freezes one yet, so there is nothing to keep
// it in. (Until this operation was served, the top of this file listed retiring a control
// among the acts with no operation, ONPLO-3051.)
//
// Corresponds with POST /controls/{control_id}/retire (the `RetireControl` operationId).
func (c *Client) RetireControl(ctx context.Context, controlId ControlId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewRetireControlRequest(c.Server, controlId)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

// SetControlStatusWithBody Say where a control stands
//
// Its own operation because it has its own rules, and a field in a patch body cannot carry
// them (decision 0547). The rules are decision 0652's.
//
// A PERSON SETS THREE OF THE FOUR WORDS: Not started, In progress and Implemented. From any
// of the three to any other, in either direction.
//
// IMPLEMENTED NEEDS PROOF. It is refused, as `evidence_required`, while the control has no
// evidence attached — the catalogue's "never marked satisfied without evidence", and
// Implemented is the word that sentence is about. What counts is what `evidence_item_count`
// counts: one piece attached today is enough, and this API judges neither how fresh it is nor
// whether it is the right proof.
//
// FAILING IS NEVER SET BY HAND. Only a check sets it, so the word always means a check found
// it. It is not among the values this operation takes, and a request that sends it is
// refused as any other value outside the list is: `unprocessable_request`, with a pointer at
// the field.
//
// ASKING FOR THE STATUS IT ALREADY HAS CHANGES NOTHING AND ANSWERS 200, so a retry of a change
// whose answer was lost is not a second change — with one exception that is the rule itself:
// asking for Implemented on a control that stands at Implemented and has no proof is refused
// like any other request for Implemented without proof. `removeEvidence` never leaves a
// control so (decision 0664); a piece of evidence that is being deleted is no proof either,
// and that is how one can still stand there.
//
// WHAT IS NOT DECLARED, AND SAID SO. Whether a person may move a control out of Failing
// (ONPLO-1016, point 13): no check is declared in this API, so nothing it can reach stands at
// `failing`. (This paragraph also named what the status becomes when the last proof is
// removed, until decision 0664 ruled it: `removeEvidence` says it.)
//
// A RETIRED CONTROL IS NOT MOVED: `control_retired`, whatever word is asked for, its own
// included (`retireControl`).
//
// THE PERMISSION IS THE ONE THAT CHANGES A CONTROL. The catalogue's row for it reads "set its
// steward and status … never marked satisfied without evidence", so the act has its row and
// this operation coins none.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with PUT /controls/{control_id}/status (the `SetControlStatus` operationId).
func (c *Client) SetControlStatusWithBody(ctx context.Context, controlId ControlId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewSetControlStatusRequestWithBody(c.Server, controlId, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

