// AddIncidentNoticeAddressFileWithBody Add a list of addresses from a Storage file to a notice to the affected people
//
// "MOST AFFECTED PEOPLE ARE NOT MEMBERS, SO A PERSON MAY ADD A LIST OF ADDRESSES FROM A STORAGE
// FILE." The file is one you can read in Storage, named by its id; Compliance reads its current
// version once, and what it proposes is the addresses it held then. Each address is proposed
// and not confirmed (`listIncidentNoticeRecipients`), so adding one changes nothing a signer
// signed: confirming them does (`confirmIncidentNoticeRecipients`).
//
// WHAT THE FILE HOLDS. "A plain-text file with one address on each line, or a CSV whose column
// headed "email" holds them; a spreadsheet is saved as CSV first." It is read as a CSV when its
// first line has a field headed `email`, in any case; otherwise as one address on each line.
// "At most 100,000 addresses and 10 MB."
//
// A LINE THAT IS NOT AN ADDRESS IS SKIPPED, NOT FATAL. "The list shows how many were skipped and
// each skipped line": `skipped_count` here, and each one, by its line number, from
// `listIncidentNoticeAddressFileSkippedLines`. An address has no space, one `@` and something
// on either side of it, as Account's rule is. A blank line is no line of the list and is not
// counted. "An address given twice, or already proposed, counts once": once is
// `already_proposed_count`, and two addresses that differ only in capitals are one.
//
// ONLY A NOTICE TO THE AFFECTED PEOPLE takes a file: your customers' recipients are the people
// who subscribed on your Trust Center, `not_to_affected_people`.
//
// REFUSED, AND WRITES NOTHING. Each of these is `422` at `/body/file_id`, its `detail` saying
// which: a file you cannot read, or that is not there; a sealed one, whose bytes cannot be read;
// one larger than 10 MB; one holding more than 100,000 addresses. A CLOSED INCIDENT IS READ AND
// NOT WORKED, `incident_closed`. Storage that could not be reached is `storage_unavailable`, and
// the same request can be sent again.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /incidents/{incident_id}/notices/{notice_id}/address-files (the `AddIncidentNoticeAddressFile` operationId).
func (c *Client) AddIncidentNoticeAddressFileWithBody(ctx context.Context, incidentId IncidentId, noticeId IncidentNoticeId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewAddIncidentNoticeAddressFileRequestWithBody(c.Server, incidentId, noticeId, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

