// ListIncidentObligations Read the reports an incident owes
//
// "THE LIST OF REPORTS THIS INCIDENT OWES, DERIVED FROM WHAT IT TOUCHED AND THE FRAMEWORKS IN
// SCOPE, NEVER TYPED FROM MEMORY." Each names the law, the body it goes to, its steps and due
// moments, its state and its steward (ONPLO-2336). Seeing them is a permission of its own
// (decision 0811). One incident in any state, closed ones too, with the nearest deadline among
// them.
//
// WORKED OUT WHEN THE INCIDENT IS WORKED. Working one (`updateIncident`) and overriding it to
// notifiable (`overrideIncidentNotifiability`) add each obligation then owed and not yet on the
// list, with the moment it was first owed. AND WHEN WHAT BRINGS ONE IS RECORDED LATER: adding a
// framework to scope (`addFramework`) and classifying a touched entry personal
// (`updateRegisterEntry`) do the same on each incident not closed, from that moment (ONPLO-3955),
// and so do classifying an AI system and saying our role towards it (`classifyAiSystemRecord`,
// `setAiSystemRecordRole`) on each incident not closed that involves it (ONPLO-3956).
// AN OBLIGATION ONCE OWED STAYS ON THE LIST: taking a touch away, lowering the severity or
// overriding the incident to not notifiable never removes one, because its clock already ran. It is dismissed with a written reason instead
// (`dismissIncidentObligation`), and a dismissed one stays on the list, Dismissed, with its
// reason. The nearest deadline leaves a dismissed obligation's steps out.
//
// A STEP IS FILED BY A PERSON OR BY A SENT NOTICE. A person records a filing with the
// authority's reference and receipt (`fileIncidentObligationStep`). Sending a notice
// (`sendIncidentNotice`) records the `notice` step of each running obligation to its audience
// at the moment it is sent, and that filing names the notice (ONPLO-3923). This said until 13
// November 2026 that sending was not built yet.
//
// THE ORDER IS FIXED: "GDPR, UK GDPR, NIS2, DORA, the CRA, the EU AI Act, then your
// customers", and within a law the authority before the affected people. (This named neither
// the CRA nor the EU AI Act until 13 November 2026.)
//
// THE CRA AND THE EU AI ACT EACH CARRY THEIR OWN TRIGGER (ONPLO-3956). The CRA's: in scope, and
// the incident's `product_affected` is not `no`, owed to `csirt_and_enisa`. The EU AI Act's: in
// scope, the incident names an AI system whose tier is `high`, and its `serious_incident` says
// a case; owed to the `market_surveillance_authority` for a system you provide, and to the
// `ai_system_provider` for one you deploy, each once, as the record's `our_role` says.
//
// NOT HERE YET, each named so a reader knows it is missing and not forgotten:
//   - HANDING AN OBLIGATION TO ANOTHER STEWARD AND MOVING THE CUSTOMERS' DUE MOMENT. Each is
//     the page's and neither is built yet (ONPLO-3954). A filing is recorded on a step
//     (`fileIncidentObligationStep`), and an obligation is dismissed with its reason
//     (`dismissIncidentObligation`).
//   - WHICH ENTRIES A FRAMEWORK APPLIES TO. No framework names its scope yet (ONPLO-1629), so a
//     framework in scope applies to every entry of the register (settled, ONPLO-3957).
//
// Corresponds with GET /incidents/{incident_id}/obligations (the `ListIncidentObligations` operationId).
