// CreateVendorWithBody Register a vendor
//
// "REGISTER A VENDOR OR SUB-PROCESSOR." Its name and its type, the one required field of
// the screen: "Name & type — Vendor or sub-processor". The type is the mark, so both are
// asked for and neither is guessed.
//
// BY HAND. "Connections marked as sub-processors appear here automatically", and no page
// says how one arrives or what it carries; nothing here makes a vendor from a connection.
//
// MARKING ONE A SUB-PROCESSOR TELLS NOBODY. "The mark is what puts it on the public Trust
// Center list", and "editing the sub-processor list then offers to send the notice". The
// public page and the notice are not declared yet (ONPLO-3190, changes 2 and 3), so no list
// is published and no notice is offered by this. (This said the Trust Center itself was not
// declared until its owner's side was, ONPLO-3190.)
//
// A NAME A VENDOR IN USE ALREADY CARRIES IS REFUSED with `name_taken`, and nothing is made.
// "No two vendors carry the same name … compared without regard to capitals or the spaces
// around it, among the vendors that are not retired, and a second one is refused with the
// vendor that holds the name shown" (decision 0779). So "the cloud host " is refused where
// "The Cloud Host" is in use, the refusal names that vendor in `name_held_by_vendor_id`, and
// a retired vendor's name is free: "a company that buys from them again registers the vendor
// anew". The name is kept as it was sent, capitals and spaces included. (This said "a name
// already in use is not refused: no page says" until that decision.)
