	// OverrideIncidentNotifiabilityWithBodyWithResponse Override whether an incident is a notifiable breach
	//
	// "WHETHER AN INCIDENT IS NOTIFIABLE IS WORKED OUT, AND A PERSON MAY OVERRIDE IT with a written
	// reason" (decision 0722, point 9). The answer and its reason are kept on the incident, and
	// "the legal clock starts when it became notifiable, whichever way that happened".
	//
	// AN OVERRIDE TO NOTIFIABLE OWES WHAT THE FRAMEWORKS IN SCOPE OWE, as if each one's trigger
	// were met, from the moment of the override: a person who says a legal clock runs starts it.
	// AN OVERRIDE TO NOT NOTIFIABLE OWES NOTHING NEW while it stands, and removes nothing already
	// owed: each obligation is dismissed with its own reason (ONPLO-3954). Both settled by the
	// spec lane on 1 November 2026, and written onto the Incidents page with ONPLO-3957.
	//
	// THE SAME ANSWER WITH THE SAME REASON CHANGES NOTHING. A second reason for the same answer is
	// kept in place of the first, and an incident that stays notifiable keeps the moment it became
	// so.
	//
	// A CLOSED INCIDENT IS NOT OVERRIDDEN, and is refused with `incident_closed`.
	//
	// IT ANSWERS WHAT IT SET: whether it is notifiable, the reason, and since when. (It answered the
	// whole incident until 16 October 2026, when whether it is notifiable left the incident.)
