	// UpdateVendorWithBodyWithResponse Rename a vendor, or mark it a sub-processor
	//
	// THE NAME AND THE MARK, AND NOTHING ELSE YET. Every field optional, an omitted one
	// untouched. Taking the mark off is the same act as putting it on.
	//
	// NOT HERE, AND THE PERMISSION COVERS EACH: "Name the register entries it touches, set or
	// override the risk tier with a kept reason, turn monitoring on, offer or sever the exchange
	// link." The entries and the tier are acts of their own under this permission,
	// `setVendorRegisterEntries` and `setVendorRiskTier` (ONPLO-2250), and the other two wait on
	// services that are not built. (This said the entries and the tier "are declared in a change
	// of their own" until 14 October 2026.)
	//
	// A NAME ANOTHER VENDOR IN USE CARRIES IS REFUSED with `name_taken`, and it keeps the name it
	// had — the rule `createVendor` states, and the refusal names the vendor that holds it. A
	// vendor's own name sent again in other capitals is its own, and is not refused.
	//
	// A RETIRED VENDOR IS NOT CHANGED: `vendor_retired`, and nothing is written. The page says
	// so: "Nothing on a retired vendor can be changed, its name and its sub-processor mark
	// included, so the auditor reads what was true the day it was retired." (This said no page
	// ruled on it until 5 November 2026; decision 0952 settled it.)
