// PickReadyMadeControlsWithBodyWithResponse Pick ready-made controls
//
// A PICK COPIES. Each ready-made control named becomes a control in this account
// environment's library: an ordinary entry, with the ready-made control's statement, standing
// at `not-started` with no evidence, exactly as one somebody wrote. "The copy records which
// ready-made control it came from and that one's version, so a newer version Onplo ships
// never changes your control by itself" — that record is `Control.picked_from`.
//
// IT ASKS WHO ANSWERS FOR THEM, AS WRITING A CONTROL DOES. "Picking several at once asks once
// for all of them; nobody is filled in": one steward, said in the request, for every control
// this pick makes. The steward is asked about as `createControl` asks (decision 0670) —
// somebody who is not in this account environment is refused `steward_not_found`, and when
// the identity service cannot be asked the answer is `identity_unavailable`.
//
// ONE OR MANY, AND A PICK OF ONE IS A PICK OF MANY THAT NAMES ONE. There is no second
// operation for "all at once": the request lists the ready-made controls, up to 500.
//
// A READY-MADE CONTROL IS IN A LIBRARY ONCE, AMONG THE CONTROLS IN USE. "A retired ready-made
// control can be picked again, as a new copy": a copy that was retired does not count, and
// picking its ready-made control again makes a new control beside it. One that is there and
// in use is SKIPPED, never
// refused and never copied twice — "picking all at once skips it" — and the answer names it
// under `already_picked`. So the same request sent again makes nothing and answers the same
// ids there: a retry of a pick whose answer was lost is not a second pick. The control that
// was picked before keeps the steward and the statement it has.
//
// ALL OF IT OR NONE OF IT. An id that names no ready-made control refuses the whole request
// with `not_found`, and nothing is picked: a pick that made some and dropped others without
// saying would leave a library nobody chose.
//
// AT MOST 100 CONTROLS A REQUIREMENT, as linking one counts them: Onplo's and a person's
// together. A pick that would give any requirement its 101st control is refused whole,
// `requirement_controls_full`, and nothing is picked.
//
// AND THE REST OF A SCENARIO KEPT. "The rest is then offered, as a second step anybody may
// skip. Picking them is the act of picking ready-made controls, and each control picked
// there is linked to the risk too." So a pick that names `risk_id` links that risk to every
// control it names: each one it made, and each it skipped as in the library already. It
// asks nothing more than picking. A risk that is not in this register refuses the whole
// pick with `not_found`, and a risk the links would take past 500 controls refuses it with
// `risk_controls_full`; nothing is picked either way. The links are written in the same
// transaction as the copies, and their audit event is the risk's.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION — `control-pick.run`, beside writing a control. A person may be allowed
// to pick what Onplo ships and not to write a control of their own.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /control-picks (the `PickReadyMadeControls` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) PickReadyMadeControlsWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*PickReadyMadeControlsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.PickReadyMadeControlsWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParsePickReadyMadeControlsResponse(rsp)
}

