// RemoveEvidenceWithResponse Remove a piece of evidence from a control
//
// IT SAYS SO AFTERWARDS. Removing an item leaves one line in the audit log naming who took it
// away and when, and the control it was proving has one piece of evidence fewer from that
// moment. A control cannot stay proven on proof that is no longer there.
//
// A FILE ATTACHED FROM STORAGE IS NOT DELETED. Its bytes are Storage's, and deleting that file
// is Storage's operation under Storage's rules. What goes here is the statement that this file
// proves this control.
//
// A FILE UPLOADED AS EVIDENCE GOES WITH ITS ITEM, because "only Compliance removes it"
// (`startEvidenceUpload`). It goes to Storage's Trash when its item goes. Storage that could
// not be asked is `storage_unavailable`, and nothing is removed: ask again.
//
// AN ITEM THAT PROVES OTHER CONTROLS TOO STAYS ON THEM. It is taken off this control alone,
// and the item goes when it proves no control any more (`attachEvidence`). Its uploaded file
// stays until then.
//
// AN IMPLEMENTED CONTROL THAT LOSES ITS LAST PROOF GOES BACK TO IN PROGRESS, IN THE SAME ACT
// (decision 0664). "Its history says why": the audit line this removal leaves also carries
// the status, from `implemented` to `in-progress`, so the two are one record and cannot be
// told apart later. Staying Implemented with a flag was refused: it "would let Implemented
// mean something without proof". A control that has proof left keeps its word, and so does a
// control that stood at anything else — removing proof from a control that is In progress
// moves nothing but the count.
//
// THE LINE IS IN THE CONTROL'S HISTORY. The page has the control's "history saying the proof
// was removed, and by whom", so the audit event this leaves is about the control: it names
// the removed item by its id, as what was there before, and nothing else of it.
//
// A RETIRED CONTROL'S EVIDENCE STAYS, "for the auditor": a removal is refused with
// `control_retired`, and nothing is removed (`retireControl`).
//
// IT IS ANSWERED WITH NO BODY, so a client that shows the control reads it again: its
// `evidence_item_count` is one fewer, and its `status` is whatever this left it at. (This
// paragraph said "WHAT IT DOES TO THE CONTROL'S STATUS IS NOT DECLARED" until that ruling: the
// page had the control "fall back to unproven", and no status is called unproven.)
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with DELETE /controls/{control_id}/evidence-items/{evidence_item_id} (the `RemoveEvidence` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) RemoveEvidenceWithResponse(ctx context.Context, controlId ControlId, evidenceItemId EvidenceItemId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*RemoveEvidenceResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.RemoveEvidence(ctx, controlId, evidenceItemId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseRemoveEvidenceResponse(rsp)
}

