// SetControlStatusWithBodyWithResponse Say where a control stands
//
// Its own operation because it has its own rules, and a field in a patch body cannot carry
// them (decision 0547). The rules are decision 0652's.
//
// A PERSON SETS THREE OF THE FOUR WORDS: Not started, In progress and Implemented. From any
// of the three to any other, in either direction.
//
// IMPLEMENTED NEEDS PROOF. It is refused, as `evidence_required`, while the control has no
// evidence attached — the catalogue's "never marked satisfied without evidence", and
// Implemented is the word that sentence is about. What counts is what `evidence_item_count`
// counts: one piece attached today is enough, and this API judges neither how fresh it is nor
// whether it is the right proof.
//
// FAILING IS NEVER SET BY HAND. Only a check sets it, so the word always means a check found
// it. It is not among the values this operation takes, and a request that sends it is
// refused as any other value outside the list is: `unprocessable_request`, with a pointer at
// the field.
//
// ASKING FOR THE STATUS IT ALREADY HAS CHANGES NOTHING AND ANSWERS 200, so a retry of a change
// whose answer was lost is not a second change — with one exception that is the rule itself:
// asking for Implemented on a control that stands at Implemented and has no proof is refused
// like any other request for Implemented without proof. `removeEvidence` never leaves a
// control so (decision 0664); a piece of evidence that is being deleted is no proof either,
// and that is how one can still stand there.
//
// WHAT IS NOT DECLARED, AND SAID SO. Whether a person may move a control out of Failing
// (ONPLO-1016, point 13): no check is declared in this API, so nothing it can reach stands at
// `failing`. (This paragraph also named what the status becomes when the last proof is
// removed, until decision 0664 ruled it: `removeEvidence` says it.)
//
// A RETIRED CONTROL IS NOT MOVED: `control_retired`, whatever word is asked for, its own
// included (`retireControl`).
//
// THE PERMISSION IS THE ONE THAT CHANGES A CONTROL. The catalogue's row for it reads "set its
// steward and status … never marked satisfied without evidence", so the act has its row and
// this operation coins none.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /controls/{control_id}/status (the `SetControlStatus` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) SetControlStatusWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, controlId ControlId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*SetControlStatusResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.SetControlStatusWithBody(ctx, controlId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseSetControlStatusResponse(rsp)
}

