//
// EVERY EXEMPTION THIS DEVICE HAS HAD, the one that stands now and the ones that ran out —
// each with its reason, its end and who granted it. An exemption is excluded from readiness
// WITH ITS REASON, in the export too, so the reason is evidence and none of them is ever
// dropped from this list.
//
// `standing` SAYS WHICH ONE COUNTS NOW. At most one does. It is "the moment has not passed",
// answered in this API's clock rather than the client's, so a client shows it and never
// works it out.
//
// DEVICES IS NOT ASKED. An exemption is Compliance's own record, so a device with none answers
// an empty page, and another account environment's device answers the same.
//
// BUSINESS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENTS ONLY. A personal account environment has no company
// compliance programme and therefore no Exempt; the permission that gates this does not
// exist there.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /device-records/{device_record_id}/exemptions (the `ListDeviceExemptions` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListDeviceExemptionsWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceRecordId DeviceRecordId, params *ListDeviceExemptionsParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListDeviceExemptionsResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListDeviceExemptions(ctx, deviceRecordId, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListDeviceExemptionsResponse(rsp)
}

// ExemptDeviceWithBodyWithResponse Exempt a device
//
// AN ADMINISTRATOR'S DECISION, CARRYING A REASON AND AN END — a kiosk, a test machine. Both
// are required: an exemption with no end is a device nobody will ever look at again, and one
// with no reason cannot be shown to an auditor. The end is a moment and the zone it was
// chosen in, as everything a person picks an end for is (decision 0661).
//
// THE WHOLE DEVICE READS `exempt` WHILE IT STANDS, in this app and in the Devices app, which
// reads the reason from here and shows it on the row. It never reads as proven: only
// `compliant` counts, and an exempt device is left out of readiness with its reason beside it.
//
// WHEN ITS MOMENT PASSES, THE DEVICE RETURNS TO WHAT ITS EVIDENCE SAYS. Nothing has to be
// called for that; the exemption simply stops standing.
//
// ONE AT A TIME. A device that already has a standing exemption is refused
// `device_already_exempt`: the reason is evidence, so an exemption is never overwritten by a
// second one.
//
// AN END THAT HAS ALREADY PASSED IS REFUSED, as a field that is wrong (`/body/ends_at`), and so
// is a zone no clock knows (`/body/ends_at_zone`).
//
// THE DEVICE IS NAMED BY ITS ID — for a device the Devices app governs, the id Devices
// minted. Exempting it changes nothing in Devices and reaches no device: this app proves,
// and never manages.
//
// DEVICES IS ASKED WHETHER THE DEVICE IS THERE, as the caller and before anything is written
// (ONPLO-1765). Another account environment's device, one being deleted and an id that names
// nothing are each `not_found`, and the caller is not told which. When Devices cannot be asked
// the answer is `devices_unavailable` and nothing was written.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /device-records/{device_record_id}/exemptions (the `ExemptDevice` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ExemptDeviceWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, deviceRecordId DeviceRecordId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ExemptDeviceResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ExemptDeviceWithBody(ctx, deviceRecordId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseExemptDeviceResponse(rsp)
}

