// UpdateFindingWithResponse Work a finding
//
// "WORK A FINDING — WHAT IS WRONG, WHERE, AGAINST, SEVERITY, STEWARD, STATE, ROOT CAUSE,
// AND A FIX-BY CHANGE THAT NEEDS A KEPT REASON." Every field optional, an omitted one
// untouched (ONPLO-1969, point 3).
//
// WHAT A FINDING SAYS MAY CHANGE AFTER IT IS RAISED: what is wrong, where it sits, what it
// breaks, its severity and its steward. A new steward is asked of identity as one named when
// the finding was raised is, and a new where or against must name an entry, a control or a
// requirement of this environment, as on `createFinding`. `against` sent as `null` names
// nothing it breaks. `where` sent as `null` sits it on nothing, which only a nonconformity
// may: a change that leaves a finding sitting on nothing and naming no requirement is refused
// 422 (`finding_where_missing`), whichever of the two fields it sent.
//
// A NEW SEVERITY RECOMPUTES FIX BY "from the moment the finding was raised, unless a person
// changed Fix by by hand": an open finding nobody re-dated is due on the day it was raised
// plus its new severity's window. One re-dated by hand keeps its date, and a closed one keeps
// the date it closed with.
//
// A FIX-BY CHANGE CARRIES ITS REASON, ALWAYS. "Changing it needs a reason, and the reason is
// kept." The date and the reason travel as one object, so a date without a reason is
// refused by the shape. The finding then answers both, and a later change replaces both.
//
// THREE STATES MAY BE SET HERE: `open`, `in-progress` and `false-positive`. A false positive
// is one "with a reason": `false-positive` sent without `false_positive_reason` is refused
// 422 (`false_positive_reason_missing`), and the reason sent with any other state, or with
// none, is refused the same way (`false_positive_reason_without_false_positive`).
//
// A FALSE POSITIVE IS CLOSED, AND "A CLOSED FINDING IS NEVER RE-DATED". The fix-by it had the
// moment it closed is the one it keeps, whatever a window does afterwards, and a `fix_by` sent
// to one is refused 422 (`closed_finding_not_redated`).
//
// A FALSE POSITIVE MAY GO BACK TO OPEN, "with a reason that is kept" (ONPLO-1969, point 5).
// `state` `open` sent to one needs `reopened_reason`, or it is refused 422
// (`reopened_reason_missing`); `in-progress` sent to one is refused 422
// (`closed_finding_reopens_to_open`), because it goes back to Open. The reason sent with any
// other change is refused 422 (`reopened_reason_without_reopening`). Opened again, its
// false-positive reason is taken away, and the date it closed with is let go: the windows
// give its fix-by again. A date a person set by hand stays, with its reason. Each refusal
// here is the field that is wrong, under the 422, and nothing was written.
//
// AN ACCEPTED RISK STAYS ONE. A finding is signed as an accepted risk by `acceptFindingAsRisk`,
// and no page lets it go back: "A finding that is Fixed or False positive may go back to
// Open", and an accepted risk is neither. So any `state` sent to one is refused 422
// (`accepted_risk_stays`), and it is never re-dated, as no closed finding is. What it says may
// still change, as a false positive's may.
//
// NOT HERE, each with what holds it:
//   - `fixed` — "with the evidence of the fix attached", an evidence item under the finding
//     (ONPLO-1969, point 1), and no evidence item can be attached under a finding yet. A
//     finding a check raised "is Fixed only when that check next passes, never by hand"
//     (decision 0804, point 1).
//   - `accepted-risk` — it is signed, under a permission of its own: `acceptFindingAsRisk`.
//   - A VENDOR OR A DEVICE AS WHERE (ONPLO-1969, point 7). `where` names an entry of the
//     register, as on `createFinding`, until a vendor and a device can be named there.
//
// Takes a body of the `application/json` content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PATCH /findings/{finding_id} (the `UpdateFinding` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) UpdateFindingWithResponse(ctx context.Context, findingId FindingId, body UpdateFindingJSONRequestBody, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*UpdateFindingResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.UpdateFinding(ctx, findingId, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseUpdateFindingResponse(rsp)
}

// AcceptFindingAsRiskWithBodyWithResponse Sign a finding's closing as accepted risk
//
// "SIGN THE CLOSING OF A FINDING AS ACCEPTED RISK — A NAMED APPROVER GOES ON THE RECORD." The
// finding stays, marked Accepted risk with who signed it, and "a matching risk with treatment
// Accept appears in Problems → Risks, linked back to it — two entries, neither a copy of the
// other" (decision 0722, point 3). The caller signs in their own name: who signed is written
// from the call's credential, never from the request. Ring 1, and never an agent.
//
// THE RISK IT MAKES. "Its sentence is the finding's What is wrong, and the signer picks its
// inherent and residual ratings while signing" (ONPLO-1969): the two ratings are this call's
// body, held to the scale as `createRisk` holds them. Its treatment is Accept, with no note,
// and "a risk made by accepting a finding carries that finding's sign-off and is not signed
// twice": the one signature this call writes is the finding's and the risk's Accept's both.
// The risk names the finding in `accepted_from_finding_id`, and the finding names the risk
// in `accepted_risk`. Both may be re-assessed afterwards like any risk.
//
// ONLY AN OPEN FINDING IS ACCEPTED: one that is Open or In progress. A finding already
// Fixed, a false positive or an accepted risk is refused `finding_not_acceptable`. It closes
// here, and "a closed finding is never re-dated": it keeps the fix-by it had the moment it
// was signed.
//
// THE RISK'S NAME MAY BE SENT, SHORTENED BY THE SIGNER. A risk is "the risk in plain language,
// on one line", of at most 500 characters; what is wrong may run to 2000 and keep its lines.
// "When the finding's words run past one line, or past what a risk's name may hold, the signer
// is shown them as the risk's name and shortens them while signing; nothing is cut silently,
// and the finding keeps its own words" (ONPLO-3799). So `name` is the risk's name as signed,
// filled from what is wrong; a name that is not one line of at most 500 characters is refused
// by its shape, 422, and nothing is written. Sent without one, the risk's name is what is
// wrong as it stands, and a finding whose words do not fit is refused
// `finding_not_a_risk_sentence`, with nothing changed. The finding's own words never change
// here (ONPLO-3821).
//
// ONE SIGN-OFF SIGNS IT, UNTIL THE COMPANY'S SIGN-OFF SETTING SAYS MORE. How many sign, from
// which group, and whether a note is required, is the setting of decision 0731, point 1,
// which is not declared on this contract yet; it joins this act the day it is, as it joins
// `signRiskAcceptance`. So "never the finding's own steward while somebody else may sign" is
// not refused yet either.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /findings/{finding_id}/accepted-risk (the `AcceptFindingAsRisk` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) AcceptFindingAsRiskWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, findingId FindingId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*AcceptFindingAsRiskResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.AcceptFindingAsRiskWithBody(ctx, findingId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseAcceptFindingAsRiskResponse(rsp)
}

