// ListFrameworksWithResponse The frameworks in scope
//
// WHAT THIS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT IS WORKING TOWARDS — "Which frameworks this environment is
// actually working towards — the choice that decides what the whole app shows you." Only the
// frameworks in scope: what the catalog offers is not this list. It is
// `listCatalogFrameworks`, under a permission of its own. (This said seeing the catalog
// "has no permission yet" until it got one.)
//
// A FRAMEWORK IS A LENS AND NEVER A SECOND LIBRARY. "Map once, prove many": every framework
// here counts controls from the one control library, so nothing on this list owns a control
// or a piece of evidence.
//
// A ROW SAYS WHAT THE FRAMEWORK IS, WHO ANSWERS FOR IT AND WHEN YOU AIM TO BE READY, AND
// NOTHING ELSE YET. Readiness is not on the row: it is counted when it is asked, and is
// `getFrameworkReadiness`. (This said "no page says how it is counted" until the Frameworks
// page did.) Its requirements are not on it either: decision 0783, point 6, says what one
// carries, and no operation reads or writes one yet. (This said no page said what one
// carries until that decision, and the row named no steward until it was given one.)
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /frameworks (the `ListFrameworks` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ListFrameworksWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *ListFrameworksParams, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ListFrameworksResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ListFrameworks(ctx, params, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseListFrameworksResponse(rsp)
}

// AddFrameworkWithBodyWithResponse Add a framework to scope
//
// A FRAMEWORK FROM ONPLO'S CATALOG, OR YOUR OWN REQUIREMENT SET, AND SOMEBODY WHO ANSWERS FOR
// IT. "Shipped (from the Control Center catalog …) or Custom (define your own requirements;
// they map to the same control library)." `from` says which: a catalog entry by its id, or
// the name of your own set.
//
// THE STEWARD IS ASKED BEFORE IT IS ADDED, NEVER AFTER (decision 0783, point 4). "A framework
// in scope with no owner and no evidence is worse than one that is honestly out of scope, so
// adding one asks who its steward is before it is added rather than after." Said here and
// never defaulted, and asked of identity as `createControl` asks (decision 0670): somebody
// who is not in this account environment is refused `steward_not_found`, and when identity
// cannot be asked the answer is `identity_unavailable` and nothing was written.
//
// IT ADDS NO CONTROL. "It then OFFERS the ready-made controls it needs, to pick one by one or
// all at once, and adds none by itself" (decisions 0664 and 0783, point 2). A control exists
// in the library only once somebody writes or picks it, so adding a framework changes
// nothing in the library and nothing in the evidence.
//
// IT OWES WHAT IT BRINGS ON EVERY INCIDENT NOT CLOSED, from the moment it is added: a
// framework that carries a duty to report adds each report it then owes to each incident that
// is not closed and is not overridden to not notifiable, as working the incident would
// (`listIncidentObligations`, ONPLO-3955). Its event names each obligation it added, by its id.
//
// THE FRAMEWORKS PAGE IS WHERE IT IS ADDED (decision 0783, point 8). Settings → Frameworks in
// scope shows the same list and links there; there is one list and this is its one door.
//
// A SHIPPED ONE TAKES THE CATALOG'S NAME, and keeps which entry it came from, which this
// answer does not carry yet. A custom one is the name you give it. Both are served since 19
// October 2026 (ONPLO-2667). (This said "NOT SERVED" until then, and that a custom one would
// be served first; the catalog's content arrived before either was.)
//
// THE SCOPE IT APPLIES TO, CHOSEN HERE. "A framework applies to one scope or to the whole
// environment, never to several. It is chosen when the framework is added": `scope_id`
// names one scope saved with `createScope`, and left out or `null` is the whole environment.
// A scope that is not here is refused `scope_not_found`, and nothing was written. It is
// changed afterwards with `setFrameworkScope` and `clearFrameworkScope`. (This said the
// scope was not here yet, and would join this body and `updateFramework` together, until
// the spec lane gave it an act of its own on 6 November 2026, ONPLO-3112.)
//
// A CATALOG FRAMEWORK IS IN SCOPE ONCE. "Adding one already in scope is refused, and the
// refusal names it": 409 `framework_already_in_scope`, with the framework in scope as
// `in_scope_as_framework_id`. Two adds of the same one at the same moment are refused at the
// database, which can name none, so the member is not required. For a second part of the
// company, change the scope it applies to. A framework removed from scope may be added again.
//
// A LAW IS FREE, A VOLUNTARY FRAMEWORK IS PREMIUM (decision 0934). "Every framework in the
// duties group is free to track, on every plan and with no limit, in a company and in a
// personal environment." A catalog entry whose `kind` is a certificate or an industry
// standard needs Premium: without Compliance's Premium seat the answer is 409 `not_in_plan`,
// with `not_included` naming the feature, and nothing was written. WHEN ACCOUNT CANNOT BE
// ASKED about the seat, the answer is 503 `account_unavailable`, never `not_in_plan` in its
// place; a law asks Account nothing. A framework of your own asks nothing here. (This said
// neither refusal was made, and that a second personal framework waited on a feature row,
// until decision 0934 settled both, ONPLO-4090: a second personal framework is refused only
// when it is voluntary.)
//
// NOT HERE YET: AUTO-MAP. Matching the controls you picked to the requirements Onplo ships
// them for is free, and needs a framework's requirements, which no operation reads or writes
// yet. Mapping the controls you wrote is the agent's Premium work, and arrives as a draft.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /frameworks (the `AddFramework` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) AddFrameworkWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*AddFrameworkResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.AddFrameworkWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseAddFrameworkResponse(rsp)
}

