// GetFrameworkReadinessWithResponse How ready one framework is
//
// HOW MANY OF ONE FRAMEWORK'S REQUIREMENTS ARE DONE, OUT OF HOW MANY ARE COUNTED —
// "Readiness counts requirements done. It is the share of the framework's requirements whose
// linked controls are all Implemented."
//
// WHICH REQUIREMENTS. A framework added from Onplo's catalog counts the requirements Onplo
// ships for that entry, each "a reference code, a statement in Onplo's own wording, the
// controls mapped to it" and its standing on the Statement of Applicability (decision 0783,
// point 6). Onplo ships them for ISO 27001 and the GDPR first; any other entry, and a
// framework of your own, has none yet and counts `0` of `0`. Served since 19 October 2026
// (ONPLO-1958). (This said "NOT SERVED, AND IT WAITS ON WHAT A REQUIREMENT IS" until then,
// and answered 501.)
//
// WHICH CONTROLS ARE LINKED. A control in your library is linked to a requirement when it was
// picked from one of Onplo's ready-made controls that Onplo maps to it. A control you wrote
// yourself is linked to nothing until somebody links it with `linkControlToRequirement`, and
// from then on it is counted. `listFrameworkRequirements` names the controls each requirement
// is counted by. (This said linking "is not served yet" until 21 October 2026, ONPLO-2893.)
//
// A RETIRED CONTROL IS NOT COUNTED. It "stops counting toward any framework: a requirement
// whose only linked control is retired counts as not done until another control is linked"
// (the Controls page).
//
// TWO COUNTS, AND THE SHARE THEY MAKE (`share_percent`). "The share is rounded down to a whole
// percent, so 100% shows only when every counted requirement is done", and with nothing to
// count it is `null`: "never 0% and never 100%". (This said how it is rounded, and what shows
// with nothing to count, was on no page until ONPLO-1957 ruled both.)
//
// WHAT IS COUNTED, AS THE PAGE SAYS IT. "A requirement excluded on the Statement of
// Applicability, with its reason, is not counted, and a requirement with no control linked
// to it counts as not done." And "A requirement a nonconformity points at is not done until
// that nonconformity is Fixed, whatever its controls say", so one closed as an accepted risk
// "still counts as UNMET on readiness" (the Findings page). A requirement is excluded with
// `excludeRequirement` and taken back in scope with `includeRequirement`. (This said
// "Nothing excludes a requirement yet" until both were served, ONPLO-2892.)
//
// THE TARGET DATE NEVER ENTERS IT. It "sits beside the number as “on track” or “behind” and
// never enters it", and `pace` says which: "behind when its target date has passed and at
// least one counted requirement is not done, and on track otherwise", and neither for one
// with no target date. The date itself is `Framework.target_date`. (This said no page said
// when it is which until ONPLO-1957.)
//
// JUDGED WHEN IT IS ASKED, and stored nowhere: a control that leaves Implemented moves the
// number at once.
//
// THE SAME PERMISSION AS SEEING THE FRAMEWORK: the catalogue's row is "See readiness per
// framework and what each requires". A framework removed from scope is not one, and answers
// `not_found`.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /frameworks/{framework_id}/readiness (the `GetFrameworkReadiness` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) GetFrameworkReadinessWithResponse(ctx context.Context, frameworkId FrameworkId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*GetFrameworkReadinessResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.GetFrameworkReadiness(ctx, frameworkId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseGetFrameworkReadinessResponse(rsp)
}

